## Installation

### 1. Clone the repository

```bash
git clone <repository-url>
cd youtube_scraper
2. Install dependencies
pip install pandas python-dotenv google-api-python-client
3. Create environment file

Create a .env file in the root directory:

touch .env

Add your API key inside:

YOUTUBE_API_KEY=YOUR_API_KEY_HERE

Important:

Do not use quotes
Do not add spaces around the equal sign
Project Structure
youtube_scraper/
├── scraper.py
├── .env
├── data/
│   └── raw/
└── README.md
How to Run

Run the scraper using:

python3 scraper.py
Output

Each run generates a new CSV file inside:

data/raw/

Example filenames:

ryan_trahan_comments_run_01.csv
ryan_trahan_comments_run_02.csv
ryan_trahan_comments_run_03.csv

Each file represents a separate dataset run and will not overwrite previous outputs.

How Randomness Works

The scraper introduces controlled randomness in two ways:

Video Selection
Videos are fetched from the channel uploads
The list of videos is shuffled before processing
Comment Selection
Each video randomly uses either relevance-based or time-based ordering
Final collected comments are shuffled before saving

This ensures variation between runs while still remaining within the same channel scope.

Configuration

You can adjust scraping behavior inside the script:

Default settings
Maximum videos: 30
Maximum comments per video: 250
Example custom run
python3 scraper.py --max-videos 60 --max-comments-per-video 300

This increases dataset size per run.

Common Issues
Missing API key

Ensure .env exists and contains:

YOUTUBE_API_KEY=your_api_key_here
No output file generated

Possible causes:

YouTube API quota exceeded
Network or API errors
No accessible videos or comments
Similar results across runs

This is expected behavior because:

The same channel is used
The same video pool is sampled
Randomness is controlled, not fully random
Notes
Only works with YouTube Data API v3
Some videos may have disabled comments and will be skipped automatically
API quota limits apply based on Google Cloud settings
Recommended Usage

For best dataset size and diversity:

python3 scraper.py --max-videos 60 --max-comments-per-video 300

This typically produces between 10,000 and 18,000 comments per run depending on video availability.

